== VERDICTS ==
X-9 (F-6 endpoint half): the three fields were NOT observed in ANY captured response.
  7 captures over 90 s while switch 9 was OFF: stale_since=0 stale_polls=0 last_error=0 in
  every one. The unreadable switch is DROPPED from the response (10 -> 9 switches), not
  served from its previous table with markers. The kernel DID detect the condition --
  '1 switch(es) unusable for 2+ polls ... down_reason=switch-unreachable' -- so this is not
  a failure to trigger. The C32 line 'serving N switch(es) from their previous flow table'
  does NOT appear in this OVS log at all.
  SCOPE LIMIT, stated rather than glossed: the trigger I could create on OVS is a
  powered-OFF switch (bridge gone), not P4's DEADLINE_EXCEEDED read failure on a switch
  that still exists. So this proves the fields are absent in the scenario reachable here;
  it does not prove they can never appear. X-9 remains UNVERIFIED-with-evidence, and the
  evidence points at 'not implemented on the endpoint'.

X-8 (A-4f OVS half): BLOCKED, and the blocker is finding 4.
  The task is 'power-cycle an OVS switch, then assert the sFlow record came back'.
  sFlow was never configured on ANY bridge on this fabric, so there is no record that
  could come back. This arm is structurally unrunnable on 'ndt up ovs4' until
  ovs_4host_topo.py configures sFlow the way testbed_topo.py:105/202 does.
  The half that COULD be run was run: the power cycle itself works (Success both ways,
  state OFF then ON, switch count 10 -> 9 -> back), and telemetry_status stayed 'unknown'
  on all 40 edges throughout -- which is the expected consequence of no sFlow, NOT an
  independent confirmation of A-4f.

== 🔴 CORRECTION 2026-09-03 00:39 -- the X-9 verdict above is SUPERSEDED by 37_x9_stale_fields_captured.log ==
The verdict above ("the evidence points at 'not implemented on the endpoint'") was reached from
the WRONG TRIGGER, and my own later experiment refutes it.
  * Here I powered a switch OFF. A powered-off switch is DROPPED from the response (10 -> 9),
    so there is no row left to carry a stale marker. That observation still stands.
  * The condition F-6 actually describes is a switch that still EXISTS but cannot be READ.
    T-7 produced it by accident: stopping Ryu made all 10 switches unreadable at once.
  * Under that condition the endpoint DOES carry the fields, on 10/10 switches:
    stale_since=1788366888, stale_polls=2, last_error='no_response'.
    Captured response saved as x9_table_response.json (16567 bytes).
=> X-9 is a PASS. What remains as a (smaller, separate) gap: a POWERED-OFF switch is dropped
   rather than served stale-marked, so the two unreachability causes are reported differently.
